INT. COPYING ROOM - MELLOR AND STROUD, LIVERPOOL - DAWN

It is 1893. There are eight desks in the room. ADA MELLOR, twenty-six, sits at one of them copying documents with a steel pen.

ADA
I have been a copying clerk in this shipping office for six years, ever since my father died. It is not a very exciting life, but it is respectable, and I need the wages.

MR. STROUD, sixty, comes in wearing his coat.

STROUD
Good morning, Miss Mellor. I am Mr Stroud, the chief clerk. I need the papers for the barque Wakefield before the tide.

ADA
I have begun them, Mr Stroud.

STROUD
Good. Please hurry.

ADA
I will hurry.

He goes out. Ada keeps copying.

INT. COPYING ROOM - MORNING

Ada copies the manifest of the Wakefield.

ADA
Iron rail. Sheet lead. Salt. Deals. Burthen four hundred and ten tons.

She writes it all down carefully and blots it with sand.

ADA (CONT'D)
There. That is finished.

She takes it to Mr Stroud.

INT. STROUD'S ROOM - MORNING

Stroud reads the copy at the window.

STROUD
This is not right. Please copy it again with this alteration.

He writes on the sheet and hands it back to her.

ADA
Yes, Mr Stroud.

She looks at the alteration.

ADA (CONT'D)
He has changed the burthen from four hundred and ten tons to nine hundred and sixty tons. That is a very large difference. I wonder why he has done that.

She goes out to copy it again.

INT. COUNTING HOUSE - DAY

CAPTAIN HARROW, fifty, stands by the fire. Stroud is with him.

HARROW
Hello, Stroud. I am Captain Harrow, the owner's agent. The Wakefield sails on Thursday.

STROUD
Yes, she sails Thursday.

HARROW
And the crew?

STROUD
She has signed on nine men instead of fourteen.

HARROW
That is not many. But it will have to do.

Ada carries a tray of ink across the room and hears all of this.

ADA
That is very strange. Nine men is not enough for a ship that size. Something is not right about this at all.

INT. COPYING ROOM - EVENING

Ada is alone with a candle. She finds her first copy in the waste basket.

ADA
Here is my first copy, which says four hundred and ten tons. And the new copy says nine hundred and sixty tons. I believe Mr Stroud may be committing a fraud on the insurers.

She looks at the paper for a long time.

ADA (CONT'D)
But I am only a clerk, and a woman, and I have no proof, and if I say anything I will lose my position and my wages, and then where would I be? I had better say nothing.

She puts the copy back in the waste basket and blows out the candle.

INT. COPYING ROOM - MORNING

Stroud comes in and looks at Ada.

STROUD
Miss Mellor, you seem troubled this morning.

ADA
I am quite well, Mr Stroud. Thank you.

STROUD
Very good. Carry on with your work.

ADA
Yes, Mr Stroud.

She carries on with her work. She copies for several hours without saying anything.

ADA (CONT'D)
I have decided that it is not my business. I am only a copying clerk and it is not my place to question the chief clerk.

EXT. SALTHOUSE DOCK - DAY

It is raining. The Wakefield is being warped out of the dock. She is sitting very low in the water.

ADA
There she goes. She is sitting very low. I hope nothing bad happens to her.

She stands and watches until the ship is out of sight.

INT. STROUD'S ROOM - DAY

Stroud is at his desk.

STROUD
Miss Mellor, the house is reducing its staff and I am afraid we no longer require you. Please leave your keys.

ADA
Very well, Mr Stroud. I am very sad about this, because I have worked here for six years and I have always tried to do my duty.

STROUD
I am sorry. That is business.

She puts her keys on the desk and leaves.

INT. COUNTING HOUSE - DAY

Captain Harrow comes in with an UNDERWRITERS' CLERK.

UNDERWRITERS' CLERK
Mr Stroud, we have received an anonymous letter about the Wakefield and there is going to be an inquiry.

STROUD
An inquiry. I see.

HARROW
These things take years, of course. Nothing may come of it.

STROUD
Nothing usually does.

They stand by the fire together.

EXT. MELLOR AND STROUD - STREET - DAY

Ada stands on the step in the rain.

ADA
Well. That is the end of that. I suppose I shall have to look for another position somewhere else. It has been a very difficult week, and I have learned that the world is not a fair place, especially for a woman without a character reference.

She puts up her umbrella and walks away down the street.
